<script>
' Chapter 2/4 — router.isBackJourney combined with an explicit focus(<id>) call in setup(), letting
' the SAME route choose a different initial focus target depending on how it was entered. Adapted
' from apps/sample-app's own DirectionalFocusDemo.thr/DirectionalFocusDemoDetail.thr (see GRAMMAR.md's
' "Router" section -> schemaless data reads for the primitives themselves) into this app's own
' chapter framing — the mechanic and the live-verified round trip it proves are unchanged.
'
' Two independent, already-documented primitives combined:
'   - router.isBackJourney — a schemaless read off the router's own current route data, exactly like
'     router.path/router.params.*/router.backJourneyData.* — `true` when this mount was reached via
'     router.back(), `false` for an ordinary router.navigate(...) (forward) mount.
'   - focus(<id>) — an EXPLICIT focus request, always winning over both the vacuum rule and any
'     automatic route-memory restoration — called from setup(), which every router-mounted component
'     gets automatically, at exactly the right point in the mount cascade for this to establish real
'     key routing.
'
' A real authoring need this solves: the SAME route can legitimately want a DIFFERENT entry point
' depending on how the user arrived — e.g. a multi-step flow that should focus its own "continue"
' action on a fresh forward visit, but its own "review/edit" action when the user has stepped away
' and come BACK to revisit it.
'
' This chapter doesn't need a separate "customized" example beyond the mechanic itself, since
' forward-vs-back IS the two-variant contrast this chapter exists to show (see
' findings/demo-app-conventions.md's rule 4 and findings/router-demo-app.md).
'
' Verify live: enter this chapter via FAST-FORWARD, confirm buttonA (yellow) is focused; press OK,
' then OK again on the detail screen's own back prompt; confirm buttonB (yellow) is focused this
' time, not buttonA, even though nothing about THIS screen's own content was ever left focused on
' the way out (buttonA itself is what triggered leaving).
scale field rootWidth: integer = 1280
scale field rootHeight: integer = 720
scale field buttonWidth: integer = 620
scale field buttonHeight: integer = 60
scale state titleTranslation: object = [40, 30]
scale state explanationTranslation: object = [40, 80]
scale state buttonATranslation: object = [40, 220]
scale state buttonBTranslation: object = [40, 300]
scale state buttonLabelInset: object = [16, 20]

public function setup() {
  if (router.isBackJourney) {
    focus("buttonB")
  } else {
    focus("buttonA")
  }
  router.markReady()
}

private function goToDetail(key: string, press: boolean) {
  if (press) {
    router.navigate("/directional-focus-detail")
  }
}
</script>

<component>
<Rectangle id="root" color="0x101010FF" width="{rootWidth}" height="{rootHeight}">
  <Label id="title" text="2/4 -- directional focus (router.isBackJourney)" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{titleTranslation}" />
  <Label id="explanation" text="Forward entry focuses A; a BACK journey into this route focuses B instead" color="0x888888FF" translation="{explanationTranslation}" />
  <Rectangle id="buttonA" focusable="true" color="0x3A3A3AFF" width="{buttonWidth}" height="{buttonHeight}" translation="{buttonATranslation}" on:key[OK]="{goToDetail()}">
    <Label id="buttonALabel" text="A -- focused on FORWARD entry. OK: go to detail screen" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{buttonLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
  <Rectangle id="buttonB" focusable="true" color="0x3A3A3AFF" width="{buttonWidth}" height="{buttonHeight}" translation="{buttonBTranslation}" on:key[OK]="{goToDetail()}">
    <Label id="buttonBLabel" text="B -- focused when returning via a BACK journey. OK: go to detail again" color="0xFFFFFFFF" translation="{buttonLabelInset}" />
  </Rectangle>
</Rectangle>
</component>
